  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 1149 PM CDT Sun Sep 27 2026\n\nTuesday through Thursday: \n\nSoutherly winds will develop on Tuesday as high pressure shifts\ntoward the lower Great Lakes and a low pressure trough\napproaches from the Northern Plains. The trough continues to\ntrend slower so the arrival time of the associated rain into\nsouth central WI by be delayed until after midnight Tue nt. The\n00Z models are hinting at the trough stalling over central WI all\nday Wednesday. This would lower our rain chances along and \nsouth of I-94 that day, but increase them for Wed nt and Thu\nmorning.\n\nThe reason for this front stalling is that an upper low will be\nsitting over the southwest U.S. and the northern stream flow \nwill not be amplified enough to kick the trough out of WI until\nThursday afternoon now. There will be a good amount of moisture\nwithin this trough with precipitable water values between 1.5\nand 2.0 inches, but the instability will be very limited due to\nweak mid level lapse rates. We could have a few rumbles of\nthunder due to the overall dynamics, but no severe storms. \n\nFriday through Sunday:\n\nStrong high pressure will move through the Great Lakes Friday\nand Saturday. We can expect cooler temperatures and dry weather\nduring this time. An upper level shortwave trough could bring a\nround of showers Saturday night or Sunday.  \n\nCronce",
